course: dictionary
lesson: 11677
title: Assured: sure and certain
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which one to use. Assured is one of these words. «She gave an assured speech.» Here the speaker was calm and sure of herself, so assured means confident.
explain: «Our win is assured.» No person is feeling confident in this sentence. The win is certain to happen, so assured means certain. Ask yourself: is someone feeling sure, or is something certain to happen?
explain: Assured can also be a verb, the past form of assure. «Mum assured me the dog was safe, so I stopped worrying.» Mum told me firmly, so I would not worry. The phrase «rest assured» means «don't worry, it is certain».
fact: «Assured» means sure and confident in yourself.
fact: «Assured» can also mean certain to happen, as in «Our win is assured.»
fact: «Rest assured» means don't worry, it is certain.
fact: Assure is a verb: when you assure someone, you tell them firmly that something is true so they will not worry.
fact: «Self-assured» means very confident in yourself.
exercise: «She gave an assured speech.» What does assured mean here? | Written down | *Confident and sure of herself | Nervous and shaking | Very long
exercise: «Our win is assured.» What does assured mean? | Lost for ever | *Certain to happen | Being shouted | Cheap to buy
exercise: Dad assured me the bus was coming soon, so I waited. What did Dad do? | Argued about the bus | Forgot the bus time | *Told me firmly so I would not worry | Ran away from the bus
test: The team felt assured of victory before the game started. What does assured of mean? | Tired after training | Happy to pay | Afraid of losing | *Certain that they would win
test: «Rest assured, the parcel will arrive on Monday.» What does rest assured mean? | *Don't worry, it is certain | Check the parcel again | Go to sleep now | Ask someone to carry it
exam: Ella says «assured» only means sure of yourself. Is she right? | No, it means very old | Yes, and it is a noun | Yes, it only ever means sure of yourself | *No, it can also mean certain to happen, as in «Our win is assured»
